Context: I am building WARCHEST, a token on Robinhood Chain (an Arbitrum-style L2)
with a custom Uniswap v4 hook + a governance system weighted by holding
seniority + a keeper that trades on Hyperliquid. The full design is in
warchest-technical-plan.pdf (attached).

RULE NUMBER ONE: before writing a single line of code, do the necessary
research and write it up in a RESEARCH.md file. I want you to verify,
not assume:
- the exact ABI and behavior of Uniswap v4 hooks on Robinhood Chain
- the permission model of Hyperliquid agent wallets (confirm that
  "trading only, no withdrawal" is actually enforced by Hyperliquid itself,
  not just a client-side convention)
- the real behavior of the Across bridge between Robinhood Chain and Hyperliquid
  (delay, cost, on realistic treasury amounts, not just trivial
  tests)
- real measured (not estimated) gas cost for the hook and for the per-wallet
  FIFO lot logic

Do not code anything until RESEARCH.md is filled in on these points.

Build order (one git branch per step, in this strict order):
1. feat/token-hook — WarchestToken.sol (standard ERC20, zero tax logic
   in it) + WarchestHook.sol (v4 hook, takes 10% on swap depending on
   buy/sell direction, sends it to the vault)
2. feat/governance-levels — WarchestGovernance.sol (levels 1 to 10 per wallet,
   vote weighted by level x supply, quorum with fallback to the previous decision)
   — test it fully with a mocked vault, it depends on nothing else
3. feat/treasury-vault — WarchestVault.sol (executes only orders
   pre-validated by governance, hard cap of 20% of treasury per trade,
   automatic stop-loss) — test with a mocked keeper
4. feat/indexer — off-chain service that computes the levels and the FIFO
   state of the lots from the on-chain history, pushes updates to
   WarchestGovernance
5. feat/keeper-hyperliquid — the most complex and the riskiest, to be done
   last: reads the votes, bridges via Across, opens/closes positions
   on Hyperliquid via an agent wallet scoped trading-only

Non-negotiable constraints:
- WarchestGovernance and WarchestVault are two separate contracts, never merged
- No tax on transfers of the token itself, only via the hook
- The Hyperliquid agent wallet must never have withdrawal rights
- Full end-to-end cycle test on testnet before any real capital

Start with RESEARCH.md.
